from pathlib import Path
import re
p=Path('scripts/literature_01_qa.py')
s=p.read_text(encoding='utf-8')
start=s.index('q(25,')
a,b=s[:start],s[start:]
changes=[
('She then attempts to control Macbeth through his ideas about courage. Challenging his manhood turns a moral decision into a test of identity.', 'She then attempts to control Macbeth through his ideas about courage: “When you durst do it, then you were a man” (1.7). Making manhood conditional on murder turns a moral decision into a test of identity.'),
('Once Macbeth becomes king, her influence weakens. He arranges Banquo’s murder without fully sharing his plans.', 'Once Macbeth becomes king, her influence weakens. Her admission “Naught’s had, all’s spent” (3.2) contrasts nothing gained with everything sacrificed: becoming queen has not brought fulfilment. Macbeth tells her to “Be innocent of the knowledge, dearest chuck” (3.2), withholding the details of Banquo’s murder. The affectionate address cannot hide her exclusion.'),
('However, Macbeth increasingly chooses further crimes as a solution. He organises Banquo’s death and orders the attack on Macduff’s family.', 'However, Macbeth increasingly chooses further crimes as a solution. “The very firstlings of my heart” (4.1) will become immediate action, replacing his earlier moral debate with unchecked impulse. He organises Banquo’s death and orders the attack on Macduff’s family.'),
('He still fights, recalling his original courage, but courage by itself does not restore moral worth.', 'His refusal “I will not yield” (5.8) recalls his original courage, but defiance by itself does not restore moral worth.'),
('His request that the stars conceal his desires shows that he understands those desires cannot be openly defended.', 'His command “Stars, hide your fires” (1.4) asks for darkness to conceal his “black and deep desires”. The imagery connects ambition with moral darkness: he understands that his wishes cannot be openly defended.'),
('Lady Macbeth strengthens the pressure by linking murder to courage. Her challenge encourages him to value proving himself above protecting a guest.', 'Lady Macbeth strengthens the pressure by declaring “When you durst do it, then you were a man” (1.7). The conditional challenge makes masculinity something Macbeth must prove through murder. She encourages him to value proving himself above protecting a guest.'),
('Macbeth increasingly acts without Lady Macbeth’s guidance. His problem has become self-sustaining: every crime creates fear that appears to require another crime.', 'Macbeth increasingly acts without Lady Macbeth’s guidance. “I am in blood” (3.4) imagines him wading through the violence he has created. He treats going on as easier than turning back, so every crime becomes an excuse for another.'),
('Immediately after Duncan’s murder, Macbeth imagines a voice announcing that he has murdered sleep. **Personifying sleep** turns a physical act of killing into the destruction of rest and innocence.', 'Immediately after Duncan’s murder, Macbeth reports hearing “Macbeth does murder sleep” (2.2). **Personifying sleep** turns a physical act of killing into the destruction of rest and innocence.'),
('Macbeth’s defensive speech suggests guilt even though the guests do not know its cause.', 'His denial “Thou canst not say I did it” (3.4) suggests guilt before anyone at the banquet has accused him. He employed the murderers, yet tries to separate himself from their act.'),
('The witches’ opening association of “fair” with “foul” unsettles clear distinctions between good and evil.', 'The witches’ opening chant “Fair is foul, and foul is fair” (1.1) unsettles clear distinctions between good and evil. The **paradox** makes apparently positive promises seem potentially dangerous.'),
('Macbeth’s dagger vision occurs as he prepares to kill Duncan. The imagined weapon appears to lead him, but he already has a plan.', 'Macbeth’s dagger vision occurs as he prepares to kill Duncan. “Is this a dagger which I see before me” (2.1) is a question, not a confident identification. His alternative description, “A dagger of the mind”, admits that the weapon may be a mental creation. It appears to lead him, but he already has a plan.'),
('Later, the apparitions encourage false security. Macbeth hears the promise about being born of a woman as a guarantee against defeat.', 'Later, the Second Apparition commands “Be bloody, bold, and resolute” (4.1). The repeated initial sound and forceful commands encourage violence and confidence. Its promise about “none of woman born” sounds to Macbeth like a guarantee against defeat.'),
('These developments expose the gap between prediction and Macbeth’s interpretation.', 'Macduff’s description “Untimely ripped” (5.8) reveals the unexpected meaning of the birth prophecy. The violent wording destroys the reassurance Macbeth took from it. These developments expose the gap between prediction and his interpretation.'),
('In Act 1, Scene 7, Macbeth decides not to proceed with murder, and she challenges his courage and love.', 'In Act 1, Scene 7, Macbeth declares “We will proceed no further in this business”, but she challenges his courage and love. “When you durst do it, then you were a man” makes her approval depend on his willingness to kill.'),
('Her claim that a little water will clear the deed contrasts with his vast images of guilt.', 'Her assertion “A little water clears us of this deed” (2.2) contrasts with his image of “all great Neptune’s ocean”. The difference in scale shows how differently they understand guilt: she sees removable evidence, while he imagines an overwhelming moral stain.'),
('He arranges Banquo’s murder while withholding details from his wife. The change in **who knows the plan** is crucial: the earlier partner is now excluded.', 'He tells her “Be innocent of the knowledge, dearest chuck” (3.2), withholding the details of Banquo’s murder. The command “Be innocent” gives her a passive role, contrasting with “partner of greatness”. The change in **who knows the plan** is crucial: the earlier partner is now excluded.'),
('Her sleepwalking reveals feelings that no longer find an ordinary conversation with him. When her death is reported, Macbeth’s response moves quickly into a bleak meditation on time and meaning.', 'Her sleepwalking command “Out, damned spot” (5.1) reveals guilt that no longer finds an ordinary conversation with him. When her death is reported, Macbeth’s “Life’s but a walking shadow” (5.5) moves into a bleak meditation on time and meaning. The insubstantial shadow suggests that the shared future they wanted has lost its value.'),
('By bringing the cost of tyranny onto the stage, Shakespeare moves beyond Macbeth’s private anguish and asks the audience to judge what his rule does to others.', 'Macduff’s cry “Bleed, bleed, poor country!” (4.3) **personifies Scotland as a wounded victim**. The repeated command expresses despair at suffering that seems endless. Alongside the attack on his family, it moves the audience beyond Macbeth’s private anguish to the public cost of his rule.'),
('Malcolm’s testing of Macduff shows greater caution than Duncan’s initial trust. His concern is whether another man can be trusted with Scotland’s future.', 'Malcolm’s testing of Macduff shows greater caution than Duncan’s initial trust. While pretending to be unfit to rule, he names the “king-becoming graces” (4.3), including “justice” and “mercy”, and claims to lack them. These qualities define kingship through moral responsibilities, contrasting with Macbeth’s concern for possession. Malcolm wants to establish whether Macduff puts Scotland before loyalty to an individual.')
]
for old,new in changes:
 assert b.count(old)==1,(b.count(old),old)
 b=b.replace(old,new)
# Make the evidence easy to locate within the connected model-essay paragraphs.
b=re.sub('“([^”]+)”',lambda m:'**'+m.group(0)+'**',b)
p.write_text(a+b,encoding='utf-8')
print('Updated all seven 25-mark essays with embedded quotations and analysis.')
